1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,618
By the end of this guide, you will know exactly what your short track blade is made of.

2
00:00:05,618 --> 00:00:10,527
You will also know whether bimetal or full PM steel suits the way you train.

3
00:00:10,527 --> 00:00:13,692
A short track blade looks like one simple object.

4
00:00:13,692 --> 00:00:16,533
A shiny runner, a tube and a couple of cups.

5
00:00:16,533 --> 00:00:21,377
Look closer and it is really two very different materials working together.

6
00:00:21,377 --> 00:00:29,709
Knowing how they fit tells you how the skate will feel in a corner, how often you will sharpen, and how long your edge will last.

7
00:00:30,409 --> 00:00:35,297
A short track blade has to do two jobs that pull in opposite directions.

8
00:00:35,297 --> 00:00:42,019
First, it has to be light and stiff, so it does not rob energy from your push or wobble under load.

9
00:00:42,019 --> 00:00:47,314
Second, it needs a hard, keen edge that bites the ice and survives sharpening.

10
00:00:47,314 --> 00:00:49,758
No single metal does both jobs well.

11
00:00:49,758 --> 00:00:55,054
Aluminium is light and stiff for its weight, but far too soft to hold an edge.

12
00:00:55,054 --> 00:01:01,979
Hardened steel holds a beautiful edge, but a whole blade made of it would feel heavy, dead and clumsy.

13
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:03,541
So the sport uses both.

14
00:01:04,241 --> 00:01:08,750
Here is how the pieces fit together, from your boot down to the ice.

15
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:10,673
At the top is your boot sole.

16
00:01:10,673 --> 00:01:17,171
Below it sit two aluminium cups, one at each end, which bolt to the boot and set the blade height.

17
00:01:17,171 --> 00:01:21,879
The cups hold the aluminium tube, which is the light body of the blade.

18
00:01:21,879 --> 00:01:27,117
Along the very bottom runs a thin strip of hardened steel, shown here in green.

19
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:31,162
That steel runner is what you skate on, and what you sharpen.

20
00:01:31,162 --> 00:01:33,947
Everything above it exists for one reason.

21
00:01:33,947 --> 00:01:41,308
It holds that steel in exactly the right place, corner after corner, while adding as little weight as possible.

22
00:01:42,007 --> 00:01:44,516
The long spine of the blade is the tube.

23
00:01:44,516 --> 00:01:48,279
How the tube is shaped is a big part of how the blade feels.

24
00:01:48,279 --> 00:01:52,732
The pillars and lips are the parts that join the tube down to the cups.

25
00:01:52,732 --> 00:02:00,321
How thick and how flexible they are decides whether the skate feels planted and direct, or soft and free under your foot.

26
00:02:00,321 --> 00:02:03,331
At each end, a cup bolts the blade to your boot.

27
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:06,216
Cup height matters more than beginners expect.

28
00:02:06,216 --> 00:02:13,617
More height lifts the blade further from the boot, so you can lean deeper into a corner before your boot touches down.

29
00:02:13,617 --> 00:02:16,941
Less height feels lower, more stable and more direct.

30
00:02:17,641 --> 00:02:21,866
When you choose a blade, you are usually offered two steels.

31
00:02:21,866 --> 00:02:26,021
Each one is quoted with a hardness number on the HRC scale.

32
00:02:26,021 --> 00:02:31,655
HRC is the Rockwell C scale, which engineers use to measure how hard a metal is.

33
00:02:31,655 --> 00:02:34,261
A higher number means a harder steel.

34
00:02:34,261 --> 00:02:39,050
Short track runners sit in a narrow band near the top of that scale.

35
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:45,459
That means a difference of just two points changes the way a blade behaves from day to day.

36
00:02:45,459 --> 00:02:51,093
So when you see 62 or 64 next to a blade, that small gap is worth understanding.

37
00:02:51,793 --> 00:02:55,291
This scale runs from 60 to 66 on the HRC scale.

38
00:02:55,291 --> 00:02:58,343
On the left, in orange, is bimetal at 62.

39
00:02:58,343 --> 00:03:02,139
It is softer, more forgiving and easier to sharpen.

40
00:03:02,139 --> 00:03:05,190
On the right, in green, is full PM at 64.

41
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:11,071
It is harder, longer lasting, and holds an edge for longer between sharpenings.

42
00:03:11,071 --> 00:03:13,676
Neither one is better in every way.

43
00:03:13,676 --> 00:03:17,769
They simply trade ease of sharpening against edge life.

44
00:03:17,769 --> 00:03:22,161
Keep this picture in mind as we look at each steel in turn.

45
00:03:22,861 --> 00:03:26,997
Bimetal is the slightly softer of the two steels, at 62 HRC.

46
00:03:26,997 --> 00:03:29,961
The word bimetal describes how it is built.

47
00:03:29,961 --> 00:03:34,097
A harder edge material is joined to a more workable backing.

48
00:03:34,097 --> 00:03:38,785
That gives you a keen edge on a body that is a touch more forgiving.

49
00:03:38,785 --> 00:03:42,162
Because it is softer, bimetal is easy to sharpen.

50
00:03:42,162 --> 00:03:49,883
If you nick an edge, or need to bring a blade back quickly between sessions, bimetal responds fast on the stone.

51
00:03:49,883 --> 00:03:54,501
Many skaters, and almost all newer racers, are happiest on bimetal.

52
00:03:54,501 --> 00:03:59,671
It asks less of your sharpening skill, and gets you back on the ice sooner.

53
00:04:00,372 --> 00:04:04,370
Full PM is a harder powder metallurgy steel, at 64 HRC.

54
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:10,551
Powder metallurgy is a way of making steel from very fine, evenly mixed metal powder.

55
00:04:10,551 --> 00:04:16,949
That produces a clean, consistent grain that can be run harder without becoming brittle.

56
00:04:16,949 --> 00:04:22,330
At 64 HRC, this steel holds an edge noticeably longer between sharpenings.

57
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:25,820
That is a real advantage if you train most days.

58
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:30,909
You spend less time at the jig, and more time skating on a fresh edge.

59
00:04:30,909 --> 00:04:32,654
The trade off is simple.

60
00:04:32,654 --> 00:04:38,398
A harder steel takes a little more work and a little more care to sharpen well.

61
00:04:39,098 --> 00:04:44,975
The honest way to choose is to match the steel to how hard and how often you skate.

62
00:04:44,975 --> 00:04:47,878
Do not just reach for the hardest number.

63
00:04:47,878 --> 00:04:57,083
If you skate a few sessions a week, and you are still building your sharpening routine, bimetal at 62 is the friendlier companion.

64
00:04:57,083 --> 00:05:03,313
If you train heavily, race often and want to sharpen less, full PM at 64 earns its keep.

65
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:04,375
One more point.

66
00:05:04,375 --> 00:05:08,624
On StayBent blades, both steels share the same flex profile.

67
00:05:08,624 --> 00:05:13,297
So you are choosing how the edge behaves, not how the blade feels.

68
00:05:13,997 --> 00:05:15,588
Steel decides your edge.

69
00:05:15,588 --> 00:05:17,379
The body decides your feel.

70
00:05:17,379 --> 00:05:25,204
Two blades can carry the same steel and skate completely differently, because of how their tube and lips are designed.

71
00:05:25,204 --> 00:05:27,326
This diagram compares two tubes.

72
00:05:27,326 --> 00:05:30,509
On top is an even tube, like the one on the QCS.

73
00:05:30,509 --> 00:05:36,742
Its stiffness is the same along the full length, which gives you stability through every push.

74
00:05:36,742 --> 00:05:40,191
Below is a tapered tube, like the one on the JaeBee.

75
00:05:40,191 --> 00:05:44,435
The top of the tube tapers down from the centre toward each end.

76
00:05:44,435 --> 00:05:47,286
So the tips have more give than the middle.

77
00:05:47,286 --> 00:05:54,581
That creates a progressive flex, and a softer, more forgiving corner for skaters who like to skate with touch.

78
00:05:55,281 --> 00:05:58,885
Your blade steel also shapes your maintenance routine.

79
00:05:58,885 --> 00:06:05,559
A softer bimetal edge comes back quickly on the stone, so a light touch up between sessions is easy.

80
00:06:05,559 --> 00:06:09,631
A harder full PM edge lasts longer before it needs attention.

81
00:06:09,631 --> 00:06:15,971
But when it does, you want good technique and clean tools, so you do not chase the edge around.

82
00:06:15,971 --> 00:06:19,242
Whichever steel you run, the basics are the same.

83
00:06:19,242 --> 00:06:22,512
Hold the blade perfectly straight while you work.

84
00:06:22,512 --> 00:06:24,114
Finish the edge cleanly.

85
00:06:24,114 --> 00:06:30,321
Then knock off the burr, which is the tiny lip of raised metal that sharpening leaves behind.

86
00:06:31,021 --> 00:06:33,039
So here is the short version.

87
00:06:33,039 --> 00:06:38,883
Start with feel, because the tube and lips decide how a blade moves under your foot.

88
00:06:38,883 --> 00:06:40,484
Then choose your steel.

89
00:06:40,484 --> 00:06:44,937
Pick bimetal at 62 if you value easy sharpening and forgiveness.

90
00:06:44,937 --> 00:06:49,529
Pick full PM at 64 if you train heavily and want longer edge life.

91
00:06:49,529 --> 00:06:55,374
Buy the blade that matches how you skate today, not how you hope to skate next year.

92
00:06:55,374 --> 00:07:01,984
The full guide, with every blade length and more detail on each model, is on the LiveLica blog.

93
00:07:01,984 --> 00:07:04,141
The link is in the description.
